Including hypermedia links can make for a very clean and elegant API response. It aligns with the Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State (HATEOAS) principle, which provides clients with all the information they need to proceed without relying on implicit knowledge of the API.

Here's a simple example of a response with hypermedia links:
```json
{
  "title": "Foo",
  "text": "Hello World",
  "_links": {
    "self": {
      "href": "/api/foos/1"
    },
    "post": {
      "href": "/api/foos/1/posts"
    },
    "author": {
      "href": "/api/users/1"
    }
  }
}
```
In contrast, a slimmer representation might look like this:
```json
{
  "title": "Foo",
  "text": "Hello World"
}
```
I'd recommend including hypermedia links in every response if your API follows the HATEOAS principle. It makes the API easier to use and more self-documenting (as the links are implicit documentation for the client).

That being said, this is a trade-off you should weigh based on your API's needs and the needs of your clients

